{
  "id": "COMPOUND:3356 - PROTEIN:1234",
  "verbalization": "Spermidine is implicated in the regulation of Potassium channel subfamily K member 3 through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Spermidine acts as a target for several compounds, including [(1S,2S,4R,7E,10R,11R)-4,8-dimethyl-12-methylidene-13-oxo-3,14-dioxatricyclo[9.3.0.02,4]tetradec-7-en-10-yl] acetate and 3-anhydro-6-hydroxy-ophiobolin A, which in turn target the same potassium channel subfamily K member 3. This indicates that Spermidine may exert its effects through a shared mechanism of action, possibly involving the modulation of potassium channels. Additionally, Spermidine is also shown to target sodium channel subunit beta-2, which is further targeted by Fuscaxanthone C, suggesting a broader role in channel regulation. The integrated evidence supports a potential mechanism where Spermidine influences potassium channel activity, possibly through the inhibition of these channels, which could have implications for cellular electrophysiology and disease states involving abnormal potassium homeostasis. The consistent targeting of potassium channel subfamily K member 3 across different compounds provides a plausible basis for the observed relationship, though further investigation is needed to elucidate the exact mechanism of action and its relevance to therapeutic applications.",
